##
## directory: container data volume, one inside each service's own directory
##
/containers/*/.data

##
## directory: build output
##
## Anchored to this stack, so they catch backend/bin and never a package that
## happens to be called bin.
##
/bin
/dist

##
## directory: source that a broader rule would otherwise swallow
##
## The repository root ignores bare directory names at any depth: bin, out,
## cache, data, build, log, logs, tmp, dist, and target. Those are aimed at
## build output, and a Go package is free to carry any of them. `internal/cache`
## is the first one this would have caught: it would have been written, tested
## green, and then skipped by git add without a word.
##
## The source trees are put back below. The patterns end in a slash so they
## match directories only, which is exactly what is needed: a directory has to
## be re-included before git will look inside it at all, and leaving files alone
## keeps *.test and the rest of the artifacts below still ignored.
##
## A rule in this file wins over the root one for every path under this stack.
##
!/cmd/**/
!/containers/**/

##
## The one exception to the re-inclusion above. Each service's data directory
## sits inside containers/, so it has to be ignored again after that rule, which
## is why this sits below it rather than with the other patterns.
##
/containers/*/.data/
!/internal/**/
!/migrations/**/
!/scripts/**/

##
## file: go build and test artifacts
##
*.test
*.prof
coverage.out
coverage.html

##
## file: local environment
##
## The settings this stack actually runs with. It holds a signing key, so it
## never leaves the machine. config.json.template is the committed copy, and the
## scripts make config.json from it.
##
.env
.env.*
config.json
!config.json.template
